ctors": ["Montana's R+10 partisan lean anchors the forecast", "No state polls, expert ratings, or market pricing are available", "National environment tilts toward Democrats, but Montana-specific signals show GOP strength", "Open-seat race with no incumbent effect"], "rationale": "Montana's deep Republican lean drives this forecast, and with no polls or expert ratings available, fundamentals carry the full weight. National signs of Democratic strength are real but have not historically translated into presidential competitiveness in a state this red.", "watch": "Any Montana-specific polling, or a national environment shift large enough to move deeply red states, would most change this outlook.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Montana remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, and this open-seat contest carries no incumbent to complicate that underlying lean. With no public general-election polls, expert ratings, or market pricing specific to the race, the forecast rests on the state's fundamentals, which point to a Republican advantage of roughly ten points.\n\nThe broader national environment offers Democrats some encouragement: recent polling shows voters wanting Congress to check the president and trusting Democrats more on issues, and the president's approval is positive in only about ten states. Montana, however, has shown durable Republican strength, including a significant GOP edge in its Senate race, and there is little evidence the state is competitive at the presidential level. The race is rated Likely Republican, with wide uncertainty given the absence of state polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1274, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.185,